From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:53:17 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-media: decoder mode: decode H.264 on two frame
 threads

A 1080p60 H.264 stream played in a WebView (High profile, one slice a
picture) takes about 1.35 times the CPU of the local test clip: DomD's
ffmpeg decodes it at 70.3 frames a second on one thread against 94.8.
On the decode thread, with the rest of DomD busy, codec in DomA
decoded 30 s of it at 60.8 frames a second without display (-S) and
played it at 50.8-59.2 (-T).

The decode thread takes its frames from libavcodec itself and the
bottom half completes them, so frame threads there only add one frame
of latency. Give a codec decoded on that thread (one without the
hwaccel: H.264) two frame threads. HEVC keeps one thread on the main
loop. Slice threads would not help: these streams have one slice a
picture.

Measured on the Raspberry Pi 5 8GB (2026-10-16), codec in DomA, 30 s
of that stream: -S 60.8 -> 75.2 fps, -T 50.8-59.2 -> 60.2-60.3; the
local clip -T 60.9; HEVC 1080p60 -S 169.5-177.8 and -T 61.1 (no
libavcodec threads for HEVC). Played from a local file in a WebView,
the stream decoded at 58.2-58.4 frames a second with its clock at 0.97
of real time; the two threads take 63-66% of a core each, against 85%
for the one thread at 47 frames a second.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c | 19 +++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c b/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c
index 3aee470..e59e4d8 100644
--- a/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c
+++ b/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c
@@ -2586,12 +2586,23 @@ static int dec_codec_open(VirtIOMediaSession *s)
         return ENOMEM;
     }
     /*
-     * One libavcodec thread. Frame-threaded decoding would reorder
-     * completions relative to the QBUF that produced them, and make libavcodec
-     * hold frames across calls in a way the buffer accounting here does not
-     * model. (q11-dec-thread gives H.264 a decode thread of its own instead.)
+     * One libavcodec thread for a codec decoded on the main loop (HEVC):
+     * completions there are produced by the QBUF that caused them, and frame
+     * threads would make libavcodec hold frames across those calls.
+     *
+     * q11-dec-frame-threads: a codec on the decode thread (H.264) gets two
+     * frame threads. Its frames already come out of that thread in decode
+     * order and are completed by the bottom half, so the one frame libavcodec
+     * holds back is only latency. A 1080p60 H.264 stream played in a WebView
+     * (High, one slice a picture, so slice threads would not help) needs about
+     * 1.35 times the CPU of the local test clip: with one thread it decoded
+     * below 60 frames a second on the Raspberry Pi 5.
      */
     d->avctx->thread_count = 1;
+    if (!d->codec->hwaccel) {
+        d->avctx->thread_count = 2;
+        d->avctx->thread_type = FF_THREAD_FRAME;
+    }
 
     /*
      * Hardware decode, FAIL-SOFT. A board whose /dev/mediaN is missing, busy or
